## Summary

`GroveDb::open` currently delegates to `grovedb-storage`'s internal `lazy_static! DEFAULT_OPTS`, which isn't exposed. This means downstream users have no way to tune RocksDB options for use cases where the defaults don't fit — embedded targets, multi-instance services, memory-constrained test environments, etc. Adding a builder that accepts tuning knobs would unblock several real-world deployment patterns.

## Today

In `grovedb-storage/src/rocksdb_storage/storage.rs`:

```rust
lazy_static! {
    static ref DEFAULT_OPTS: rocksdb::Options = {
        let mut opts = rocksdb::Options::default();
        opts.create_if_missing(true);
        opts.increase_parallelism(num_cpus::get() as i32);
        opts.set_allow_mmap_writes(true);
        opts.set_allow_mmap_reads(true);
        opts.create_missing_column_families(true);
        opts.set_atomic_flush(true);
        opts
    };
}
```

`RocksDbStorage::default_rocksdb_with_path` is the only public constructor; the options struct isn't reachable. `GroveDb::open` wraps that without exposing options either. Anything that wants a different memory or concurrency profile has to fork the crate.

## Use cases that benefit

1. **Memory-constrained deployments** — embedded targets, mobile clients, sidecar processes, containerised environments with strict memory limits. The current per-instance footprint (writebuf × max_buffers × N column families + block cache) is generous; some deployers want to opt down.

2. **Multi-instance services** — applications that run many GroveDb instances simultaneously (per-tenant databases, sharded storage backends, per-namespace stores). N instances × per-instance footprint gets expensive fast with the current defaults.

3. **Heavy test suites** — workspaces that spawn hundreds of distinct GroveDb instances during `cargo test`. `cargo nextest` mitigates this with process-per-test isolation, but the underlying configuration gap remains.

4. **Workload-specific tuning** — read-heavy applications that would benefit from a larger block cache and smaller write buffers, or write-heavy pipelines that want the opposite. `increase_parallelism(num_cpus)` and the mmap settings are also defaults a deployer might reasonably want to override per-environment.

## Proposed API

A builder that exposes the common tuning knobs while keeping correctness-required options (atomic flush, column-family creation) under grovedb's control:

```rust
pub struct GroveDbBuilder { /* private */ }

impl GroveDbBuilder {
    pub fn new() -> Self { ... }
    pub fn write_buffer_size(self, bytes: usize) -> Self { ... }
    pub fn max_write_buffer_number(self, n: i32) -> Self { ... }
    pub fn block_cache_size(self, bytes: usize) -> Self { ... }
    pub fn parallelism(self, threads: i32) -> Self { ... }
    pub fn allow_mmap(self, enable: bool) -> Self { ... }
    pub fn open<P: AsRef<Path>>(self, path: P) -> Result<GroveDb, Error> { ... }
}
```

`GroveDb::open` stays as the default-options constructor, so existing users see no change. The builder keeps `rocksdb::Options` out of the public API surface, so grovedb retains flexibility to swap rocksdb versions without breaking callers.

